  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1150 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nToday - Saturday: Light Rain\n\nAfter a dry day yesterday, high pressure moves off to the east \nduring the day today. At the same time southwesterly winds in the \nlow to mid level winds return as theta-e advection pushes it way \ninto the region starting this afternoon. With this flow regime, \nmoisture increases with PWATs roughly between 1 to 1.25\" and \ncombining this moisture with the forcing moving into the region, \nscattered rain showers will likely form beginning this afternoon and \ncontinuing into the overnight. Heading into Saturday, more rain \nshowers are expected (60 to 80%) during the morning and early \nafternoon with the coverage being more than today. Total rain \namounts are generally under 0.25\" for most locations with portions \nof northeast Iowa, far southwest Wisconsin, and southeast Minnesota \nthat could see more than 0.25\" (20 to 40%) due to the showers \nstaying in tact for longer periods of time compared to locations \nfurther to the northeast. Focusing on the Halloween evening \nforecast, there still remains a low chance (10 to 20%) for some \nlingering rain showers to occur, however most areas should remain \ndry. It may end up being more of a cool and dry evening with \ntemperatures in the low to mid 50s for any trick-or-treaters than a \nrainy one.   \n\nNext Week: Warmer and Wet Early with Cooler Temperatures and Lower \nRain Chances for the Second Half of the Week\n\nShortwave ridging remains in place for Sunday and into Monday while \na longwave trough moves in behind this ridging for early next week. \n Within this trough there are a few disturbances to watch, but the \nmore impactful one for our area would be a low pressure system that \ndevelops in the central Plains and lifts northward into the Upper \nMidwest on Monday and lingers through Wednesday. This system will \npotentially be one of the stronger storms we have had this fall. \nNAEFS PWATs are in the 99th percentile to the maximum percentile for \nthis time of year. Current forecasted PWATs are in the 1.4 to 1.7\" \nrange. Dewpoints will get into the 60s for some locations which is \nquite abnormal for this time of year. Now all this being said, these \ningredients will help in putting2 the area in a prolonged \nperiod of rain. The main question is how much rain exactly will \nfall. The better moisture is more than likely going to stay off \nto our west, however if things can come to fruition and the \nbetter moisture can stick around a little longer, resulting in a\nmore easterly push, than some of the ensemble outliers of 2 to \n3\" will be possible, but at this time this remains a low chance \nof occurring (10%). \n\nTemperatures for next week will start out quite warm for early \nNovember, with highs in the mid 60s and lows in the mid 50s, with \nsome upper 50s possible for Monday night, which is warmer than our \naverage high by several degrees! After the system moves out, \ntemperatures will cool down closer to normal with highs in the 50s \nand lows back into the 40s.",
